```js
app.get('/user/:id', (req, res) => {
  db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [req.params.id], (e, r) => {
    if (e) return res.status(500).json({ error: 'Internal error' });
    res.json(r);
  });
});
```
(Use `$1` placeholder syntax instead of `?` if you're on `pg`/Postgres.)

## 2. No error handling
The callback ignores `e` entirely. If the query fails, `r` will be `undefined` and you'll send back `res.json(undefined)` (empty 200 response) instead of surfacing the failure. Always check `e` first and return an appropriate status code.

## 3. No input validation
`id` isn't validated as a number before use. Even with parameterized queries, malformed input will just result in an empty result set (harmless), but validating early gives clearer error responses (e.g., 400 for a non-numeric id) instead of a silent empty/200.

## 4. Returns full row set / no not-found handling
`SELECT *` leaks all columns (e.g., password hashes if present) — select only needed columns. Also, if no user matches, `r` is likely `[]`, and you're returning `200 []` instead of `404`.

## 5. Leaking whether a record exists differently
Not urgent, but worth considering: returning raw DB rows directly to the client without shaping the response can leak internal fields not meant for API consumers.

